Audit this circuit for vulnerabilities:

pragma circom 2.0.0;

template SquareRootTemplate() {
   signal input x;
   signal input y;

   y * y === x;
}

Vulnerability: Square root ambiguity in a finite field.

The constraint y * y === x only says y is a square root of x. In a finite field, a nonzero quadratic residue typically has two roots: y and -y. One equation does not pick which root you mean.

Impact: If the protocol needs a canonical root (e.g. the one in the lower half of the field), a prover can often use the other root unless you constrain y further. That can break uniqueness assumptions used in nullifiers or state updates.

Fix: Add a constraint that forces y into one branch, e.g. y less than p/2 using a comparator gadget (as in circomlib).

Corrected code:
pragma circom 2.0.0;
include "comparators.circom";

template SquareRootTemplate() {
   signal input x;
   signal input y;

   y * y === x;

   component lessThan = LessThan(252);
   lessThan.in[0] <== y;
   lessThan.in[1] <== 10944121432399673150034386095624770886016148064353456036486307527127454957569;
   lessThan.out === 1;
}

